      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] On the morning of November 14, 1987, rain fell sideways down the Rue des Bains in Geneva, tapping against the green-painted doorframe of Horlogerie Vaucher like an impatient customer. Inside, twenty-two-year-old Claire Rochat sat on a three-legged stool, her eye-loupe wedged against her right cheekbone, her breath held steady as she lowered a hairspring no thicker than a spider's thread into a brass balance cock. [BEAT] The shop smelled of whale oil, damp wool, and old paper. Above her head, forty-two wall clocks ticked in slightly different rhythms, a soft wooden chorus that had kept time in that narrow room since 1924. [PAUSE 1.5s] Across the room, her master, seventy-one-year-old Henri Vaucher, was not at his bench. He was at the front counter, staring at a red-stamped notice from the municipal tax office. Quartz watches from Tokyo and Hong Kong had flooded the department stores two blocks away\u2014accurate to a second a month, cheap enough to throw away when the battery died. Nobody wanted to pay eighty francs to have a mechanical mainspring cleaned. [LOWER VOICE] Henri folded the paper twice, slipped it into his vest pocket, and said nothing. Claire knew what the silence meant. By Christmas, the landlord would take the keys, the forty-two clocks would be auctioned in lots, and [EMPHASIS: nothing inside a watch moves by accident]\u2014except, it seemed, the slow, quiet ending of a life's work. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] The next morning, Claire tried to save the shop the only way a twenty-two-year-old knew how: she walked. She took a leather satchel of Henri's finest restored pocket watches and climbed the hill toward the Rue du Rh\u00f4ne, where the grand jewelry houses gleamed behind brass grilles. At the first boutique, a manager in a charcoal suit barely glanced at the velvet tray. \"Mademoiselle,\" he said, tapping the glass counter, \"our clients buy heritage with a warranty, not repairs from a side street.\" [BEAT] At the second shop, a buyer offered her forty francs apiece for the lot\u2014less than the cost of the mainsprings Henri had hand-blued. By four o'clock, her shoes were soaked through and her satchel was just as heavy as when she left. When she pushed open the green door of Horlogerie Vaucher, Henri was already wrapping two wall clocks in newspaper. [LEAN IN] \"I told you,\" Henri said, not unkindly, \"[EMPHASIS: you cannot mend a century with a discount].\" Claire set the satchel down on the bench. \"Then we don't sell them,\" she replied, her voice tighter than she intended. \"We repair what they can't.\" Henri's jaw set. \"They don't want repairs, Claire. They want new.\" He turned back to the shelves, his shoulders rounded under his cardigan, and for the first time Claire noticed how thin his wrists had grown. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] Three nights later, the radiator clanked once and died. Outside, sleet ticked against the glass. Claire stayed at the bench past midnight under a single goose-neck lamp, determined to finish a 1924 Patek Philippe pocket watch that a retired tram conductor had left behind six weeks earlier. The watch had stopped dead at 4:12. Claire had stripped it down twice, ultrasonically cleaned every jewel, and oiled the pivots, yet every time she wound the crown, the balance wheel shuddered twice and froze. [BEAT] Her eyes burned. Her fingers cramped around the brass tweezers. At 1:15 a.m., she dropped the escape wheel\u2014a piece smaller than a lentil\u2014onto the floorboards. She fell to her knees, sweeping her palm across the cold planks, her throat tight with a sudden, humiliating heat. She was twenty-two. Her classmates from the horology school were already working in clean, fluorescent-lit factories in La Chaux-de-Fonds, earning steady salaries with dental plans. Here she was, crawling in the dust for a dead shop and a stubborn old man who wouldn't even let her turn on the space heater. [STILLNESS] [PAUSE 2s] When her fingertips finally brushed the tiny steel gear near the baseboard, she sat back against the counter and stared at the dark room. For the first time, she wondered if loyalty was just another word for being too afraid to leave. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] At 6:30 a.m., Henri found her asleep with her forehead resting on her folded arms. He didn't wake her. He simply brewed chicory coffee on the hotplate, placed a chipped mug beside her elbow, and picked up the 1924 pocket watch. When Claire blinked awake, Henri was holding the movement up to the gray window light, his loupe pressed to his eye. \"Look here,\" he murmured. Claire slid closer. Under the ten-power lens, the escape wheel looked intact\u2014until Henri tilted it a fraction of a millimeter toward the glass. One of the fifteen microscopic teeth had a hairline burr, invisible from above, catching the pallet fork on every fourth beat. [SLOW DOWN] \"[EMPHASIS: The tooth was not broken\u2014it was waiting],\" Henri said softly. \"You cleaned the whole watch, Claire, but you never tilted the light.\" [BEAT] Claire stared at him. \"How do you know to look from the side?\" Henri set the movement down and looked at her\u2014really looked at her\u2014for the first time in weeks. \"Because in 1953, I spent four days on a watch just like this, and I threw away the wrong part. My master made me sweep the floor for a month until I learned to change my angle.\" [WARM SMILE] Claire looked from the tiny gear to the stack of unpaid bills by the register. \"Henri,\" she said, \"the grand houses don't want our watches. They want what we know. Every hotel concierge in Geneva has guests whose heirloom watches stop working on holiday. Nobody in those glass boutiques will touch a movement older than ten years.\" Henri frowned. \"Hotels?\" \"Not the managers,\" Claire said, leaning forward. \"The concierges. The people who actually solve problems.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [WARM SMILE] That afternoon, Claire didn't carry a velvet tray. She carried the 1924 pocket watch\u2014running smoothly now, its tick crisp as a raindrop\u2014and a small stack of cream cards Henri had printed twenty years earlier. She walked into the lobby of the H\u00f4tel d'Angleterre, bypassed the front desk, and spoke to the head concierge, a silver-haired man named Monsieur Delacroix. She didn't ask for a favor. She showed him the watch, explained the hairline burr, and promised forty-eight-hour turnaround for any guest's mechanical timepiece. [SPEED UP] Two days later, a Belgian diplomat's 1948 chronograph arrived at Rue des Bains. A week after that, a Zurich banker's Cartier tank. By December 20, the tax notice was paid in full, and Henri had to buy a second bottle of whale oil. [SLOW DOWN] [STILLNESS] Three years later, when Henri finally hung up his apron, the sign above the green door still read Horlogerie Vaucher, with a smaller brass plaque bolted neatly underneath: *Claire Rochat, Master Watchmaker*. [PAUSE 2s] She never became famous. She never built a factory. She simply proved, one tilted light at a time, that [EMPHASIS: precision is just patience with a sharper edge].",
