      "annotatedScript": "At four-fifteen in the morning on November fourteenth, Astoria Harbor smelled of wet cedar, diesel, and cold river mud. [SCENE SHIFT] Captain Thomas Callahan stood on the steel bridge wing of the harbor tug Kestrel, his gloved fingers resting on the damp railing. At fifty-eight, Thomas had spent thirty-two years guiding grain freighters and timber barges across the mouth of the Columbia River. His routine never varied: black coffee in a dented thermos, a check of the tide tables pinned above the chart desk, and a slow walk around the main deck before the rest of the port woke up. [BEAT] That morning, however, the harbor was buried under a tule fog so thick that the sodium lights along Slip Twelve looked like smudged coins pressed into wool. Visibility had dropped below forty yards. Worse, his regular mate and chief deckhand were both laid up with a seasonal flu that had swept the union hall. Standing in the wheelhouse behind him were two replacements who had never worked a winter fog together: Maya Lin, twenty-four, three months out of maritime academy with crisp creases still in her foul-weather gear, and Devon Okafor, a twenty-six-year-old engineer who knew every bolt on the twin diesels but had barely twenty hours on the bow line. [PAUSE 1.5s] At four-thirty, the harbor radio crackled. A container feeder, the Nordic Tern, had lost her bow thruster two miles upriver and was drifting toward the shoals near Tongue Point. She needed a tug on her port quarter before the ebb tide swung her sideways into the pilings. [EMPHASIS: the fog does not care how many years you have held the license], Thomas murmured to himself, stepping inside and pulling the brass hatch shut.\n\n[SPEED UP] Within ten minutes, the Kestrel cleared the slip, her twin screws churning black water into foam. Thomas took the center helm, watching the radar sweep paint ghostly green arcs across the glass. Maya stood by the port-side searchlight; Devon waited aft by the tow winch, his handheld radio clipped to his collar. [BEAT] The first problem arrived before they even cleared the breakwater. A commercial crabber, running dark without its transponder, cut across their bow out of the whiteout. Thomas threw both throttles into reverse. The Kestrel shuddered, dishes rattling in the galley below, as the crabber's hull scraped past ten feet off their starboard fender. [LOWER VOICE] \"Captain, I didn't see him until he was on top of us,\" Maya said, her voice tight against the glass. \"My eyes are watering from staring at the glare.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Thomas didn't look up from the compass. \"Turn off the searchlight, Maya. It's bouncing back in your face.\" \"If I turn it off, we're blind,\" she replied. \"If you leave it on, we're blinded,\" Thomas said evenly. [BEAT] Ten minutes later, they reached the Nordic Tern, a dark wall of steel looming out of the mist. Devon heaved the messenger line twice, and twice the wet wind carried it short of the freighter's rail. On the radio, the Tern's master snapped, \"Kestrel, we are losing water under the keel. If you cannot hold station, tell me now so I can drop the hook.\" Devon's voice came back over the deck channel, ragged and apologetic. \"Sorry, Cap. The line's soaked, and the deck's slick as grease. I can't get the arc.\" [STILLNESS] Thomas felt the tug's stern drift toward the freighter's prop wash, and for the first time in a decade, his own shoulders locked.\n\nBy five-ten, the ebb tide was running at four knots, and the Kestrel's primary radar display flickered twice and went dead\u2014a salt-water short in the masthead cable. [PAUSE 2s] [LOWER VOICE] In the sudden dark of the wheelhouse, only the amber glow of the depth sounder remained. Thomas's knees ached from standing braced against the roll. Every instinct in his body told him to call the dispatch office, abort the assist, and let the Coast Guard towboat forty minutes away take the blame if the freighter grounded. He had a clean record. Thirty-two years without a hull claim. If he pushed forward with a blind radar and a green crew, one wrong rudder order would put two vessels on the mud before sunrise. [LEAN IN] Maya gripped the edge of the chart table, her knuckles white. \"Captain, we have no sweep,\" she whispered. \"Should I call the Tern and tell them we're backing off?\" [BEAT] Devon climbed the ladder into the wheelhouse, dripping rain onto the rubber mat, his chest heaving. \"I can bypass the breaker, Cap, give me ten minutes\u2014\" \"We don't have ten minutes,\" Thomas said. He looked at the two of them\u2014young, soaked, terrified of failing him, and waiting for him to either rescue them or bench them. [EMPHASIS: steady is a choice you make before your hands stop shaking].\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Thomas stepped away from the center helm. [PAUSE 1.5s] \"Maya,\" he said, his voice dropping to a conversational calm that seemed to shrink the wheelhouse. \"Take the wheel.\" She stared at him. \"Captain, I've never conned in zero-zero fog.\" \"You're not conning blind,\" Thomas said, pointing to the paper chart taped beside the binnacle. \"You're reading the depth sounder. Every three seconds, call out the feet under the keel. Devon, go back to the bow. Don't throw the line. Walk it to the rail, wait for the Tern's heave, and tie it off like you're bolting a cylinder head.\" [WARM SMILE] Devon blinked, then nodded once, his posture straightening. \"Aye, Cap.\" [BEAT] Thomas moved to the open bridge wing, where the fog hit his face like cold needles. He didn't touch the throttles. Instead, he listened. \"Forty-two feet,\" Maya called from inside, her voice steadier now. \"Thirty-eight feet,\" she called three seconds later. Thomas closed his eyes and heard the low, rhythmic groan of the Tongue Point bell buoy off their port beam, then the dull slap of river chop against the freighter's steel hull thirty yards ahead. [EMPHASIS: you do not steer the river; you listen to it], he called back through the open door. \"Two degrees port, Maya. Easy.\" \"Two degrees port,\" she echoed, and the Kestrel's bow answered smoothly. Out on the foredeck, Devon didn't rush. When the freighter's heavy heaving line whistled out of the mist, he caught it cleanly, hauled the hawser hand-over-hand, and dropped the eye over the bitt with a sharp metallic clack.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At five-forty-five, just as the eastern sky turned the color of wet slate, the fog began to lift in ragged ribbons. The Nordic Tern was safely moored at Pier Two, her master leaning over the bridge wing to wave a coffee mug in salute. [PAUSE 1.5s] Back at Slip Twelve, Thomas cut the engines, and the sudden quiet of the harbor settled over the Kestrel. Maya was still standing at the helm, her hands resting lightly on the spokes, as if she couldn't quite believe she had brought forty thousand tons of steel home without a scratch. Devon sat on the aft bitt, coiling the wet hawser with slow, deliberate loops. [BEAT] Thomas poured three cups from his dented thermos and carried two out to the deck. Nobody gave a speech. Nobody clapped. [LOWER VOICE] Maya looked up at the pier lights, which were finally visible through the clearing mist. \"I thought we were going to lose her when the radar died,\" she said quietly. Thomas handed her the steaming cup. \"You didn't,\" he said. [EMPHASIS: the lanterns were already lit]\u2014\"you just had to turn off the glare so you could see them.\" [PAUSE 2s] Three years later, when Maya Lin stood at a podium in Portland to accept the Port Authority's Young Mariner Award, she didn't talk about her academy grades or her license exams. She talked about a November morning in Astoria, a dead radar screen, and a captain who stepped back from the wheel so she could learn she was ready.",
