      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] November in Providence smells like wet granite and harbour salt. Inside the Aldridge Municipal Gallery, however, the air is kept at a strict sixty-eight degrees and forty-five percent humidity, humming through brass vents that have not changed since 1924. [BEAT] Clara Vance, forty-two years old, chief curator of American works, liked that hum. It meant nothing was warping. Nothing was cracking. Nothing was lying to her.\n\nFor twenty-two months, Clara had been assembling the centennial exhibition: forty-one oil paintings by New England dockworkers, lighthouse keepers, and self-taught maritime artists. The centerpiece\u2014loaned from a private estate in Newport\u2014was a four-foot canvas titled *Low Tide at Narragansett*, attributed to Arthur Pendelton, a celebrated nineteenth-century marine painter whose name alone had convinced three major donors to underwrite the gala. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nOn Wednesday morning, seventy-two hours before Friday night's black-tie opening, Clara walked into the conservation lab with a mug of black tea and a routine checklist. The painting sat on the steel easel under a bank of daylight-balanced lamps. She only meant to verify the frame's corner joins. Instead, she noticed a faint, glassy halo around the lower-right signature\u2014*A. Pendelton, 1887*.\n\n[LOWER VOICE] She set the tea down. She reached for the handheld ultraviolet lamp, clicked off the overhead fluorescents, and swept the violet beam across the corner of the canvas. [PAUSE 2s] Under UV light, old natural resin varnish glows a milky, even green. Modern synthetic retouching shows up as a dark, dead smudge. Around the signature, the milky green stopped in a neat rectangle, and inside that rectangle, [EMPHASIS: the varnish was eighty years older than the ink]. Someone had scraped away an earlier name, painted *A. Pendelton* in its place, and sealed it over with fresh damar.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By noon, Clara was standing in the mahogany-paneled office of Julian Mercer, the museum's executive director. Julian did not look up from the seating chart for Friday's dinner until she laid the UV photograph on his blotter.\n\n\"Tell me that's a restoration note,\" Julian said.\n\n\"It's a forgery,\" Clara replied. \"The brushwork in the sky is too loose for Pendelton. The rigging is wrong for his 1880s period. Whoever painted *Low Tide* knew how to mix zinc white with cobalt, and they knew how to read a tide table, but they weren't Arthur Pendelton.\"\n\n[SPEED UP] Julian leaned back, steepling his fingers. \"Clara, the invitations are printed. The *Journal* ran a four-page preview on Sunday. Three hundred trustees and donors are flying in for a Pendelton.\"\n\n\"They're flying in for a great painting,\" she said. \"They just don't know whose yet.\"\n\n\"If you pull the centerpiece three days before opening, the board will ask why our chief curator didn't catch this two years ago.\" His voice dropped, smooth and immovable. \"The estate believes it's authentic. Leave the wall label as it is. After the gala, we can quietly 're-evaluate' it in the spring.\"\n\n[STILLNESS] Clara looked at the seating chart\u2014at the little gold-inked place cards waiting to be set on linen. \"That's [EMPHASIS: a lie that everyone agrees to admire], Julian.\"\n\n\"It's survival,\" he said. \"Don't burn down the museum to prove you're clever.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At eleven-thirty that night, the gallery was empty except for the hum of the climate vents. Clara sat on a rolling stool in front of *Low Tide at Narragansett*, a magnifying loupe pressed to her eye. Her shoulders ached. Her phone had buzzed twice with messages from the registrar asking if the press packets were cleared for printing. [BEAT] If she signed the clearance form, Friday night would be effortless. Champagne, applause, a promotion she had been quietly promised in January. If she refused, she would spend the next decade known as the curator who embarrassed the Aldridge.\n\n[SLOW DOWN] She tilted the UV lamp one more time, studying the scraped rectangle. Beneath the forged *A. Pendelton*, in the microscopic ridges where the original paint had been thinner than the canvas weave, a single letter remained intact. A small, stubborn *M*. Beside it, the ghost of a vertical stroke.\n\nClara stood up, walked to the archival cabinets along the east wall, and pulled the 1890s port-authority ledgers she had collected for the exhibition's background notes. [SPEED UP] For three hours, she cross-referenced dockside painters, amateur marine clubs, and estate inventories from Newport to Bristol. At 2:40 a.m., her finger stopped on a faded receipt from a Providence frame-maker dated October 1888: *One gilt frame, 48x36, for Miss Margaret Halstead, Narragansett.*\n\n[WARM SMILE] Margaret Halstead. A lighthouse keeper's daughter who had taught herself to grind pigments from oyster shells and slate, and whose work had never once hung in a museum because nineteenth-century dealers would not buy marine oils signed by a woman. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At eight o'clock Thursday morning, Julian Mercer found Clara in the main gallery, standing beside a ladder. The *A. Pendelton* wall label was gone. In its place was a fresh piece of museum board, printed in clean black type: *Low Tide at Narragansett, 1887. Attributed to Margaret Halstead (1854\u20131919).*\n\nJulian's face tightened. \"Clara, we discussed this.\"\n\n[LEAN IN] \"Look at the painting, Julian,\" she said quietly. \"Really look at it. The light on the pilings. The way she caught the green under the wave.\" She handed him the 1888 frame receipt and the UV print. \"Someone scraped her name off this canvas to sell it for ten times the price. If we put Pendelton's name back on that wall tomorrow night, we're not protecting the museum. We're finishing the theft.\"\n\nJulian stared at the receipt for a long, silent moment. [PAUSE 2s] \"The donors will be furious,\" he said, though his voice had lost its edge.\n\n\"Some of them,\" Clara agreed. \"And the rest will realize they're looking at a discovery nobody else in New England has seen in a hundred and thirty years. [EMPHASIS: Whoever painted the harbor deserved the wall].\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Friday night arrived with rain against the tall gallery windows and three hundred guests filling the marble foyer. When Julian stepped up to the brass lectern to welcome the crowd, Clara stood near the back, her hands clasped tightly behind her back. She had packed a small cardboard box under her desk that afternoon, just in case.\n\nJulian cleared his throat. \"Ladies and gentlemen,\" he said, \"a museum's job is to preserve what matters. Three days ago, our chief curator discovered that the centerpiece of this exhibition did not belong to the famous name on its frame.\" A murmur rippled through the room. Julian looked toward the back wall, met Clara's eyes, and smiled. \"[EMPHASIS: The truth doesn't ruin a legacy; it finally gives it weight]. Tonight, for the first time in over a century, Margaret Halstead takes her rightful place.\"\n\n[STILLNESS] [PAUSE 2s] Nobody walked out. By nine o'clock, the crowd around *Low Tide at Narragansett* was three deep. An art historian from Brown University stood in front of the new label for ten minutes, taking notes. A young woman in a borrowed blazer\u2014one of the museum's unpaid interns\u2014asked Clara if she could photograph the plaque for her grandmother.\n\n[SLOW DOWN] [WARM SMILE] Long after the last glass was cleared, Clara walked through the quiet gallery one final time. The vents still hummed at sixty-eight degrees. On the wall, under the softest spotlight in the room, Margaret Halstead's name sat in plain black ink\u2014no varnish over it, no borrow, no disguise. Because [EMPHASIS: a name is a promise, not a decoration], and the only honor worth keeping is the one that can stand in the light.",
