      "annotatedScript": "On the third Thursday of November, rain came sideways off Casco Bay and rattled the tall casement windows of Bellweather and Associates. [PAUSE 1.5s] Inside, on the fourth floor of a brick warehouse in Portland, Maine, twenty-six-year-old Nadia Kowalski sat alone at a maple drafting table with a lukewarm mug of black tea and a set of blue-line prints for the Heron Point Municipal Pier. [BEAT] Nadia was six months out of graduate school. Her desk held three sharp mechanical pencils, a brass scale ruler her father had given her the day she passed her licensing exam, and a stack of municipal soil logs that smelled faintly of salt and damp paper. [SCENE SHIFT] The Heron Point Pier was supposed to be a straightforward job. A hundred and eighty feet of precast concrete deck, supported by fourteen timber-and-concrete bents, stretching out into a working harbor forty miles up the coast. The town council had already scheduled the ribbon-cutting for June. [LOWER VOICE] All Nadia had to do was verify the piling lengths on the final drawing set before the senior partner signed the seal at five o'clock. [BEAT] She started at Bent One, closest to the granite seawall, and worked her way out toward deep water. [SPEED UP] One through ten matched the 1998 town survey to the inch. Eleven, twelve, thirteen\u2014clean, predictable, boring. [SLOW DOWN] Then she reached Bent Fourteen, the outermost support at the end of the pier, and her pencil stopped. [PAUSE 1.5s] On the 1998 survey, the seabed under Bent Fourteen was listed as dense glacial till at twenty-two feet. Solid enough to hold a bridge. [LEAN IN] But wedged at the bottom of the reference folder was a folded, tea-stained sounding chart from 1974. And in the upper-right margin, written in a cramped, faded hand, was a single note: [EMPHASIS: Scour pocket east of the channel marker. Soft clay to thirty-four feet.] [PAUSE 2s]\n\nNadia stared at the note for a long time. [BEAT] If the 1974 chart was right, the forty-foot pilings specified on the new blueprint would stop six feet short of solid rock. In calm summer water, nobody would ever know. The deck would sit level, the paint would dry, and the town would applaud. [LOWER VOICE] But in January, when a nor'easter drove twelve-foot swells into the harbor, Bent Fourteen would settle, twist, and pull the outer span into the bay. [SCENE SHIFT] She gathered the folder, walked down the narrow hallway to Arthur Vance's office, and knocked on the open doorframe. Arthur was fifty-eight, wore his reading glasses low on his nose, and had signed off on more coastal structures than Nadia had seen in textbooks. [BEAT] \"Arthur,\" she said, spreading the two sheets side by side on his desk. \"Look at Bent Fourteen. The 1998 survey shows till at twenty-two feet, but this seventy-four sounding chart marks a scour pocket twelve feet deeper.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Arthur didn't look up from his email right away. When he finally leaned over the desk, he tapped the 1998 stamp with his pen. \"That's a municipal survey, Nadia. Certified. The seventy-four sheet is a harbor master's sketch from before you were born.\" [BEAT] \"I know,\" she said. \"But scour pockets don't fill themselves in. If anything, fifty years of winter tides would make it deeper.\" [SLOW DOWN] Arthur sighed, took off his glasses, and rubbed the bridge of his nose. \"The town's bond closes tomorrow at noon. If we change the piling schedule now, we add twelve feet to every outer pile, delay the order by two weeks, and trigger a full council review. You're reading ghosts into old ink. Sign the check-sheet.\" [PAUSE 2s]\n\nBy nine o'clock that night, the fourth floor was empty except for the hum of the radiator and the rain against the glass. [LOWER VOICE] Nadia sat back at her maple table, staring at the check-sheet. One signature. Five seconds of ink, and she could go home, sleep, and be the reliable junior engineer who didn't make waves. [BEAT] Her stomach tightened. She thought about her first week at the firm, when Arthur had told the new hires, [EMPHASIS: \"The stamp on a drawing isn't a decoration. It's a promise to people who will never know your name.\"] [PAUSE 1.5s] She pulled the 1974 chart closer. Beneath the margin note were three initials: T.H. [BEAT] She opened the town's public archive on her laptop, scrolled through decades of harbor records, and found the name: Tomas Herrera, Harbor Master at Heron Point from 1968 to 1991. [SPEED UP] At eleven-forty, Nadia packed her bag, grabbed her raincoat, and drove forty minutes up Route 1 through sheets of coastal rain. [SCENE SHIFT] She parked near the Heron Point seawall at six-fifteen the next morning, just as the sky turned the color of wet slate. A single yellow bulb burned inside the bait shack at the end of the granite pier. [BEAT] Inside, an older man in a waxed canvas jacket was coiling tarred line beside a kerosene heater. \"Mr. Herrera?\" Nadia asked. [WARM SMILE] He looked up, squinting through the steam of his coffee mug. \"Depends on who's asking.\" She laid the 1974 chart on the bait counter. He didn't even need his glasses. He ran a calloused thumb over the faded pencil mark and let out a short, dry laugh. [EMPHASIS: \"I wrote that the winter the Maryellen dragged her anchor,\"] he said. \"The surveyor in ninety-eight never took a boat out past the channel buoy. He shot his lines from the seawall at dead low tide. [SLOW DOWN] Every lobsterman in this harbor knows there's a soft hole out at the end of that point. Nobody drops a mooring there.\" [PAUSE 2s]\n\nNadia drove back to Portland with Tomas Herrera's faded logbook sitting on her passenger seat. [BEAT] At eight-thirty, she walked into Arthur Vance's office without knocking. She didn't lead with an apology. She placed the 1974 chart, the 1998 survey, and Herrera's handwritten sounding log in a neat row across his blotter. [LEAN IN] \"The ninety-eight surveyor shot from the seawall,\" Nadia said, her voice steady. \"He never sounded the outer channel. If we drive forty-foot pilings at Bent Fourteen, we are building a pier for calm water.\" [STILLNESS] Arthur didn't speak for a full ten seconds. He picked up the logbook, turned two salt-stiffened pages, and looked at the dates. Then he looked up at Nadia. [LOWER VOICE] \"How much longer?\" he asked. \"Twelve feet,\" she said. \"Fifty-two-foot prestressed concrete on the outer four piles. I already ran the load transfer.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Arthur reached for his red pen, crossed out the original piling schedule, and wrote the revision in clean block letters. \"Call the town manager,\" he said. \"Tell him we're delaying the bond by six days.\" [SCENE SHIFT] The Heron Point Pier opened in late June under a bright, indifferent sun. [WARM SMILE] The town council gave speeches. Nobody mentioned Bent Fourteen, or a fifty-year-old pencil note, or a junior engineer who had driven through a November gale to find a retired harbor master. [SLOW DOWN] Two winters later, a January nor'easter tore three boats off their moorings and sent nine-foot waves crashing over the granite seawall. The next morning, the harbor was littered with broken traps and splintered planking\u2014[EMPHASIS: but the Heron Point Pier stood dead level.] [PAUSE 2s] That Friday, Arthur Vance walked over to Nadia's drafting table. He didn't have a trophy or a plaque. He set a small, heavy brass plumb bob on the corner of her blotter\u2014the one he had carried in his own coat pocket for thirty years. [LOWER VOICE] \"Good work, Nadia,\" he said, and walked back down the hall. [STILLNESS] Because in the end, the real measure of any craft isn't the applause on a sunny day in June. It is [EMPHASIS: the work that holds when no one is looking.] [PAUSE 1.5s]",
